WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:02.840
It's not just in your head.

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.160
When it comes to how we all experience life

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.640
bad is generally stronger than good.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.400
We remember trauma more than we remember joy,

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.980
we're brought down by criticism more than we're elevated by praise,

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.940
and we pay more attention to bad news than good.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.760
A new book called The Power of Bad

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.420
by journalist John Tierney and psychologist Roy F. Baumeister

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.780
explores the negativity effect,

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.300
or the universal tendency for negative events and emotions

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.020
to effect us more strongly than positive ones.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.040
The negativity effect shapes everything we do

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.260
from our personal relationships, to how we do our careers,

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.340
to how we vote, to what media we consume.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.620
But the Power of Bad  isn't just one more cause for despair.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:49.180
The book's subtitle is 'how the negativity effect rules us and how we can rule it.'

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.300
And it offers practical tips on all sorts of ways to approach life

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.560
so that we can be happy, productive and well adjusted.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.260
I sat down with John Tierney, a contributing editor at the Manhattan Institute's City Journal

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:04.160
and a former New York Time's columnist and reporter

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.520
to talk about the root causes of the negativity effect and how to combat it.

